# 1. Primary Storage

Primary storage is the memory that the CPU can access directly. It stores data and instructions that are currently being used by the computer.

## Characteristics

- Fast access speed
- Volatile (most types lose data when power is turned off)
- Limited storage capacity
- Directly accessed by the CPU

## Types

### RAM (Random Access Memory)
- Temporary memory
- Stores data while programs are running
- Data is erased when the computer is turned off

### Cache Memory
- Extremely fast memory
- Located close to or inside the CPU
- Stores frequently accessed data to improve performance

### Registers
- Smallest and fastest storage inside the CPU
- Holds instructions and data currently being processed

---

# 2. Secondary Storage

Secondary storage is used for permanent data storage. Unlike primary storage, it retains data even when the power is turned off.

## Characteristics

- Non-volatile
- Larger storage capacity
- Slower than primary storage
- Used for long-term data storage

## Types

### Hard Disk Drive (HDD)
- Magnetic storage device
- High capacity
- Lower cost
- Slower than SSD

### Solid State Drive (SSD)
- Flash memory-based storage
- Faster than HDD
- More reliable
- Lower power consumption

### Optical Storage
- CD
- DVD
- Blu-ray Disc

### Flash Storage
- USB Flash Drive
- Memory Card
- External SSD

### Cloud Storage
- Stores files on remote servers
- Accessible through the internet
- Examples include Google Drive, OneDrive, and Dropbox

---

# Primary vs Secondary Storage

| Feature | Primary Storage | Secondary Storage |
|---------|-----------------|-------------------|
| Speed | Very Fast | Slower |
| Volatility | Mostly Volatile | Non-Volatile |
| Capacity | Smaller | Larger |
| CPU Access | Direct | Indirect |
| Cost | More Expensive | Less Expensive |
| Examples | RAM, Cache, Registers | HDD, SSD, USB, Cloud Storage |

